    correlate a user's events across records without the logs naming them.

  My recommendation: [1mallowlist, with identifiers truncated[0m. There are only about four call sites in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, so the cost
  of the strict option is close to zero here, and the artifact is something users hand to you — that's exactly where
  fail-open is the wrong default. The password should never be passed to the logger at any level.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the logger handle sensitive data? → Allowlist + truncated IDs (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Redaction set: allowlist, with usernames reduced to a short hash.

  Next: how the logger module gets into the page. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with a plain [38;5;153m<script src>[39m, there's no bundler,
  and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies or build step. I want to keep it that way — adding a toolchain to a two-file
  webapp is not worth it — but there's still a real fork.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m uses [38;5;153mimport[39m.
    Buys: no globals, explicit dependency, the modern default, and it's what you'd want if this app ever grows. Costs:
    module scripts are blocked by CORS over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m by double-clicking it stops working — you'd
    need to serve the directory ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar). Also defers execution, which is fine here.
  - [1mClassic script + global[0m — [38;5;153m<script src="logger.js"></script>[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, exposing a single [38;5;153mwindow.AppLog[39m. Buys:
    keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m working, zero changes to how you run the app, matches the existing style exactly. Costs: one global,
    and no import graph to read.

  Which is right depends entirely on how you actually open this app. If you already serve it over HTTP, modules are the
  better call; if you open the file directly, modules will break your workflow on day one.
